        "<strong>Y’hoshu‘a (<em>יְהוֹשֻׁעַ</em>) — “Yahweh saves.”</strong> The Hebrew name from which Greek <em>Iēsous</em> (Jesus) descends. Joshua is the first person in the Bible to bear what is, etymologically, the name of Jesus. The book opens with a deliberate compositional move: <em>after the death of Moses</em>, exactly the cadence the Pentateuch closes with (Deut 34:5). The prose is signaling that this book continues the story rather than starting a new one — the Deuteronomistic History (Joshua–2 Kings) reads as a single literary arc.",
