    (
        5,
        2,
        "",
        "Make flint knives, and circumcise",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Mass circumcision at Gilgal.</strong> The wilderness generation had not been circumcised (5:5) — a striking detail that the text explains by reference to ongoing journey conditions. The mass rite at Gilgal (“rolling place,” a wordplay on the “rolling away” of Egyptian reproach in 5:9) marks the threshold from wilderness wandering to covenantal land-life. The use of <em>flint</em> knives — a Bronze Age anachronism by the late settlement period — preserves an ancient ritual conservatism, like the use of unhewn stones for altars in Exod 20:25.",
        "User original",
    ),
