        "a man stood in front of him with his sword drawn",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The commander of Yahweh’s army.</strong> One of the more enigmatic theophanies in the Hebrew Bible. The figure is called “commander of Yahweh’s army” (5:14), refuses both human sides of the question (“Are you for us or for our adversaries?” — “No”), and demands Joshua remove his sandals (5:15) — a direct echo of Moses at the burning bush (Exod 3:5). Christian tradition reads the figure as a pre-incarnate Christ; rabbinic tradition reads him as the angel Michael. The narrative leaves the question deliberately open.",
        "User original",
