        "",
        "the wall fell down flat",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Jericho.</strong> Excavations at Tell es-Sultan (the site of ancient Jericho) have produced a famously contested archaeological record. Kathleen Kenyon’s mid-20th-century excavations dated the destruction layer to ~1550 BCE — significantly before any plausible Joshua timeframe (~1400–1200 BCE depending on chronology). Bryant Wood’s reanalysis in the 1990s argued for a later date (~1400 BCE) based on pottery typology, more consistent with the biblical narrative. The matter remains unsettled. Whatever the historical layer, the textual narrative is theologically focused: the wall falls because Israel marches and trumpets, not because they breach it — the city is given, not taken.",
        "User original",
