    (
        7,
        21,
        "",
        "a beautiful Babylonian robe",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Achan story.</strong> A counter-narrative to Rahab. Where the Canaanite woman is saved with her household, the Israelite man is destroyed with his. Both stories turn on the question of <em>ḥerem</em>: Rahab is preserved <em>from</em> the ban by oath; Achan violates the ban and falls under it. The two stories together suggest that the narrative’s moral lines run through individuals, not through ethnic categories. The Hebrew of v. 21 specifies a “Shinar mantle” — Shinar being the biblical name for Babylonia, an early instance of the “Babylon as locus of luxurious idolatry” motif that recurs throughout scripture.",
        "User original",
    ),
