        "<strong>Manuscript witness.</strong> en in the fifth century, with three columns to the page, and having (what is very unusual in early manuscripts) punctuation, accents, and breathings by the -- original scribe. It contains Gen. 31. 15 Josh. 12. 12, with many losses, however, from mutilation, and small fragments of Isaiah A and Malachi. Its evidence is valuable, and where and B differ it generally agrees with A. G. Codex Sarravianus, at L",
        "Frederic G. Kenyon, *Our Bible and the Ancient Manuscripts* (Eyre & Spottiswoode, London, 1895). Public domain.",
