    (
        13,
        1,
        "",
        "old and advanced in years",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The unfinished conquest.</strong> Chapter 13 marks a decisive narrative turn: Joshua is old, the land is not all conquered, and Yahweh tells him to assign the unconquered territories anyway. The book’s second half (chs 13–24) is concerned with land allotment, not with battles. The honest acknowledgment that significant Canaanite presence remained becomes a key theme of the book of Judges, which opens by listing precisely the territories Joshua failed to take. The conquest narrative is internally self-correcting — the Bible itself complicates the simple picture often associated with Joshua.",
        "User original",
    ),
