    (
        24,
        29,
        "",
        "Joshua the son of Nun, the servant of Yahweh, died",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The closing.</strong> Joshua dies at 110 — the same age as Joseph (Gen 50:26), one of several literary symmetries between the two figures. The book closes with three burials: Joshua’s, Joseph’s bones (carried up from Egypt as promised in Gen 50:25), and Eleazar’s. The triple burial closes the Pentateuchal patriarchal arc and prepares the ground for the Judges era — when, the next book opens (Judg 2:10), <em>another generation arose who didn’t know Yahweh, nor the work which he had done for Israel</em>.",
        "User original",
    ),
    # ---- expansion (May 2026) ----
    (
        3,
        5,
        "",
        "Sanctify yourselves",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        "<strong>Hitqaddashu (<em>הִתְקַדָּשׁוּ</em>).</strong> The reflexive of <em>qadash</em>, “make oneself holy.” In priestly contexts (Lev 11:44) holiness is a state cultivated through ritual purity; here on the eve of the Jordan crossing, the people are charged with the same disciplinary preparation soldiers undergo before holy war (Deut 23:9–14). The verb’s repetition across the conquest narratives signals that the campaign is framed not as land-acquisition but as ritual procession.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        6,
        17,
        "",
        "Only Rahab the prostitute shall live",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Rahab’s covenant.</strong> The instruction to spare Rahab is given in the same breath as the order to <em>ḥerem</em> the city. The book of Joshua holds these two together without resolving them — covenantal loyalty trumps ethnic boundary, and yet ethnic boundary is the announced rationale for ḥerem. The book’s ethics are not as flat as later readers sometimes assumed; the text itself complicates the rules it states.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        8,
        1,
        "",
        "go up to Ai",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The second attempt at Ai.</strong> The Ai narrative is unusually long (chs 7–8) for a single town because of its theological work: Achan’s sin must be exposed and resolved before Israel can advance. The site of biblical Ai is contested — et-Tell, the most-discussed candidate, was unoccupied in the conquest period — and excavators have proposed alternative identifications (Khirbet el-Maqatir most prominently). As with Jericho, the archaeology and the text don’t cleanly align; reading both together remains an open scholarly question.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        9,
        15,
        "",
        "made a  covenant with them, to let them live",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Gibeonite deception.</strong> The Gibeonites pretend to be from a far country to extract a covenant of peace, exempting themselves from the ḥerem of nearby cities (Deut 20:16–18). When the deception is exposed, Joshua honors the oath anyway (9:19) — covenants once sworn before Yahweh cannot be broken even when extracted by trickery. The principle resurfaces dramatically in 2 Sam 21, where Saul’s breach of this very covenant brings famine on Israel a generation later.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        11,
        20,
        "",
        "Yahweh to harden their hearts",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Hardening the heart, again.</strong> The same theological framing as Pharaoh in Exodus (4:21, 7:3, etc.). Israelite narrative attributes ultimate causation to God even where human agency is real. Modern readers tend to want a clean separation; the Hebrew Bible consistently refuses one. Compare 2 Sam 24:1 vs. 1 Chr 21:1 (Yahweh / Satan) — the narrators are willing to use either framing because the underlying theology of dual causation is unsystematic by design.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        17,
        3,
        "",
        "had no sons, but daughters",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Zelophehad’s daughters, again.</strong> The case from Numbers 27 is invoked as precedent: the five daughters of Zelophehad receive their inheritance in the actual land allotment. The narrative confirms that the legal innovation of Numbers actually went through — a small but meaningful detail. The Hebrew Bible is unusual in returning to specific legal cases by name across multiple books, treating them as binding precedent.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        20,
        4,
        "",
        "declare his case in the ears of the elders",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The cities of refuge in operation.</strong> Joshua’s allotment chapters realize the Numbers 35 / Deut 19 institution. The cities — Kedesh, Shechem, Hebron west of the Jordan; Bezer, Ramoth, Golan east — are spaced to keep any Israelite within a day’s flight from refuge. The institution’s practical sophistication (assembly trial, burden of proof on the killer, distinction between intentional and accidental homicide) is one of the most advanced legal frameworks of the ancient Near East.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        22,
        28,
        "",
        "the pattern of Yahweh’s altar",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The altar by the Jordan.</strong> The trans-Jordanian tribes build a large altar; the cis-Jordanian tribes mobilize for civil war, suspecting apostasy. Resolution comes through a delegation and discussion: the altar is a memorial, not a sacrifice site (22:26–28). The episode is one of the few Old Testament cases where threatened intra-Israel violence is averted through dialogue. The Deuteronomistic insistence on <em>one</em> sanctuary (Deut 12) makes the question of additional altars instantly explosive.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        24,
        2,
        "",
        "Your fathers lived of old time beyond the River",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The patriarchs in idolatry.</strong> Joshua’s farewell speech opens with an admission rarely highlighted: Israel’s ancestors served other gods before the call of Abraham. Genesis is silent on Terah’s religion, but Joshua (and Jewish midrash later, expansively) takes for granted that Abraham came out from idolatry rather than from prior monotheism. The implication: covenant election is not the recovery of an original Israelite identity but the creation of one out of pagan stock.",
        "User paraphrase; references midrash",
    ),
