    (
        4,
        9,
        "",
        "twelve stones in the middle of the Jordan",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The two memorials.</strong> The chapter records two stone-monuments commemorating the Jordan crossing: twelve stones taken from the riverbed and set up at Gilgal (4:8, 20), and a parallel set placed in the riverbed where the priests stood (4:9). The doubling has long puzzled commentators — the underwater set is invisible once the river resumes flowing — and many critical readers (so Noth, Soggin) treat 4:9 as preserving an older Gilgal cult-etiology incompletely harmonized with the dominant Gilgal-stones tradition. Theologically, the two sets work together: the visible monument at Gilgal becomes the catechetical site (4:21–24, "when your children ask"), while the submerged stones inscribe the crossing into the very ground of the river — a permanent witness regardless of human attention. The duplication is one of the OT’s small, deliberate excesses: the redundancy is itself the testimony.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        7,
        25,
        "",
        "Why have you troubled us? Yahweh will trouble you today",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The pun on Achor.</strong> Joshua’s judgment plays on Achan’s name and the resulting place-name "Valley of Achor": the Hebrew root <em>‘kr</em> ("to trouble") generates "trouble-er" (Achan) and "Place of Trouble" (Achor) — the locale becomes a permanent linguistic monument to the violation. The execution is stark: stoning, burning, more stoning, with Achan’s family and livestock included (under the assumption that the contamination of <em>ḥerem</em> goods spreads through the household). Hosea will later invert the place-name: "I will give her the Valley of Achor for a door of hope" (Hos 2:15) — eschatological reversal of the place where covenant breach was punished into the place where covenant restoration begins. The OT preserves both directions of the etymology, holding judgment and mercy in the same toponym.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        9,
        23,
        "",
        "wood cutters and drawers of water",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The Gibeonite settlement.</strong> Joshua’s curse on the deceiving Gibeonites — perpetual servitude as wood-cutters and water-drawers for the sanctuary — institutionalizes their Israelite incorporation at the lowest cultic-economic rung. The arrangement carries forward: 2 Sam 21 narrates Saul’s violation of the oath sworn to them and David’s required atonement; Ezra 2:43 and 8:20 list the <em>n’tinim</em> ("the given ones") among returnees from exile, plausibly the Gibeonites’ liturgical successors. The chapter is one of the OT’s most morally complex texts on covenant, oath-keeping, and incorporation: a treaty obtained by deception is honored because the oath was taken in Yahweh’s name (9:18–20). The principle — that an oath sworn in good faith binds even when the basis is false — runs counter to ANE legal practice and registers an early Israelite theology of the irrevocable nature of vows pronounced before the deity.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        11,
        6,
        "",
        "hamstring their horses and burn their chariots",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Hamstringing the horses.</strong> The command to disable the captured horses and burn the chariots is theologically calibrated rather than militarily wasteful: chariots and horses were the elite military technology of the era (cf. Egyptian and Hittite chariot corps), and their possession by Israel would substitute reliance on weapons for reliance on Yahweh. Deut 17:16 explicitly forbids the future king to multiply horses; David later partially complies (2 Sam 8:4, hamstrings most chariot horses but keeps 100); Solomon abandons the principle entirely (1 Kgs 10:26–29, mass importing of Egyptian horses and chariots). Joshua’s obedience here — destroying the most valuable spoil rather than absorbing it into Israel’s military — sets a benchmark that the monarchy progressively fails. The verse is a quiet anticipation of the Deuteronomistic critique of the kings’ military buildup.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        12,
        9,
        "",
        "the king of Jericho, one",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The catalog of thirty-one kings.</strong> The chapter’s formal list — thirty-one defeated city-kings, named individually with the laconic formula "one" appended — preserves the political geography of late Bronze Age Canaan with surprising precision. Most of the cities named (Jericho, Ai, Jerusalem, Hebron, Lachish, Gezer, Megiddo, Hazor, etc.) are archaeologically attested as Canaanite city-state capitals in the Late Bronze / early Iron I horizon, each governed independently — exactly the political fragmentation reflected in the Amarna correspondence (14th c. BCE), where Canaanite city-rulers individually petition the Egyptian pharaoh. The literary form of the list (king-by-king, with the repeated "one") echoes Egyptian royal triumph-inscriptions; the chapter is using a recognized ANE genre. Whether the historical pattern of conquest matches this catalog is hotly debated (Albright vs. Alt vs. Mendenhall vs. Finkelstein), but the political tableau the list assumes — many small kingdoms, no Egyptian over-presence — is consistent with the actual political reality of the relevant period.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        13,
        14,
        "",
        "no inheritance to the tribe of Levi",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Levi’s missing inheritance.</strong> The Levites receive no contiguous tribal territory; their "inheritance" is the offerings of Yahweh (here and in 13:33; cf. Num 18:20, Deut 18:1–2). The arrangement is theologically and politically calibrated: Levi’s scattering across forty-eight cities (Josh 21) embeds priestly-instructional presence in every tribal area, and the absence of a Levitical territorial bloc prevents the priesthood from accumulating a political base. The structure becomes one of the OT’s great institutional innovations — a priestly class supported by the entire confederation rather than by control of land — and it preserves a meaningful church-state separation across the tribal era. The price is Levitical economic vulnerability: when the offering system is disrupted (cf. Neh 13:10, Levites returning to their fields because tithes have stopped), the institution’s viability collapses immediately.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        14,
        6,
        "",
        "You know the thing that Yahweh spoke to Moses",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Caleb’s claim at Gilgal.</strong> Caleb invokes the divine promise from Num 14:24, made at Kadesh forty-five years earlier: because Caleb (alone with Joshua, of the wilderness generation) trusted the spies’ minority report, he was promised a portion in the land. The verse is one of the OT’s clearest cases of long-deferred personal promise: forty-five years of wilderness wandering and conquest separate the oath from its fulfillment. Caleb’s extraordinary status — at 85 he is still strong "to go out and to come in" (14:11) — is the narrative reward for forty-five years of waiting. The episode also registers the chapter’s central principle: territorial allotment is not arbitrary distribution but the working out of accumulated covenantal obligations, with named persons receiving named promises across generational distance.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        15,
        18,
        "",
        "She got off her donkey",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Achsah’s springs.</strong> Caleb’s daughter, given to Othniel for the capture of Debir, dismounts to ask her father not just for land (the dry Negev field already promised) but also for water sources — the upper and lower springs (15:19). The episode preserves a rare narrative window on female property-claim in pre-monarchic Israel: Achsah negotiates directly with her father over the conditions of her dowry, and the request is granted in full. Her dismounting is significant — descending from one’s donkey to make a request was a recognized gesture of formal petition (cf. Abigail’s similar dismounting in 1 Sam 25:23). The Caleb-Achsah-Othniel triangle is one of the OT’s small portraits of effective female agency within the patriarchal property system, and it directly precedes Othniel’s appearance as the first major judge in Judg 3 — the marriage produces the deliverer.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        16,
        10,
        "",
        "didn’t drive out the Canaanites who lived in Gezer",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The unfinished conquest.</strong> The chapter’s closing verse is one of seven similar admissions sprinkled through Joshua and Judges 1: a tribe’s conquest is incomplete; Canaanites remain. The narrative tension with chapters like 11:23 ("Joshua took the whole land") is real and editorial. Most scholars (so M. Noth) read the conquest narrative as compressing decades or centuries of intermittent and incomplete settlement into a stylized conquest-rhetoric, with verses like 16:10, 17:12–13, 19:47 preserving the historical residue. Gezer specifically remained Canaanite until Solomon’s reign (1 Kgs 9:16), when the pharaoh of Egypt captured it and gave it to Solomon as his daughter’s dowry — a four-century gap between this verse and the city’s eventual incorporation. The geography of incomplete conquest sets up the entire deliverer-cycle of Judges.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        17,
        14,
        "",
        "Why have you given me just one lot and one part for an inheritance",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Joseph tribes’ complaint.</strong> Ephraim and Manasseh — the two Joseph-derived tribes — protest that their allotment is inadequate for their numbers. Joshua’s response is structurally significant: rather than redistribute, he tells them to clear the forested hill country themselves, including dispossessing the Canaanites who hold the iron chariots in the valleys (17:18). The episode is one of the OT’s frankest acknowledgments of demographic-territorial pressure within the tribal system, and Joshua’s reply embodies a recurring biblical pattern: divine gift of land does not exempt the recipients from labor and risk. The Joseph tribes’ complaint will produce, centuries later, the political base of the northern kingdom — Ephraim’s sense of insufficient honor and territory will be the structural grievance Jeroboam I exploits at the schism in 1 Kgs 12.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        18,
        1,
        "",
        "set up the Tent of Meeting there",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The move to Shiloh.</strong> The Tent of Meeting — the wilderness sanctuary, with the Ark inside — relocates from Gilgal (the original conquest base camp) to Shiloh, in central Ephraim. The shift inaugurates roughly three and a half centuries of Shiloh as Israel’s premier cult site, until the Philistine destruction of Shiloh in the eleventh century BCE (cf. Jer 7:12, 26:6, where the prophet repeatedly invokes Shiloh’s ruin as a warning to Jerusalem; archaeological excavation at Khirbet Seilun has confirmed the eleventh-century destruction layer). The verse is the institutional founding of the Shiloh sanctuary, where Eli will preside at the opening of 1 Samuel and where Hannah will dedicate Samuel. The choice of central Ephraim (rather than Jerusalem or Hebron) reflects pre-monarchic geography: the central hill country was the sociological core of early Israel, and Shiloh’s position made it accessible from all the tribal territories.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        19,
        50,
        "",
        "Timnathserah",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Joshua’s own inheritance.</strong> The chapter ends with Joshua receiving his personal inheritance — but only after every other tribe has received theirs. The order is theologically calibrated: Israel’s leader takes his portion last, after seeing every other family settled. The town he selects, Timnath-serah ("portion of the sun" or "extra portion"), in the hill country of Ephraim, becomes his burial site (Josh 24:30). The implicit contrast with later monarchical practice is sharp: David takes Jerusalem first (2 Sam 5), Solomon builds his palace before Yahweh’s temple (1 Kgs 7), Ahab seizes Naboth’s vineyard for landscaping (1 Kgs 21). Joshua’s last-place inheritance is the inverse — the leader who gives before he receives, who builds his city only after every other tribe has its city. The pattern becomes a long-running counter-image to royal land-greed.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        21,
        45,
        "",
        "Nothing failed of any good thing",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The covenant fulfillment formula.</strong> The verse is a Deuteronomistic theological summary: every promise Yahweh made to the patriarchs about the land has been kept. The formula is rhetorically absolute ("not one word"), and its placement is structural — it closes the conquest-and-allotment narrative arc that began in Josh 1, before the farewell-and-renewal section of chs 22–24. The same formula recurs at 23:14 ("not one word has failed of all the good words"). The deuteronomistic editor frames the conquest as a complete divine performance, even as the same book has admitted (16:10, 17:12, etc.) that the conquest was practically incomplete. The theological judgment overrides the geographical reality: from the editor’s perspective, what Yahweh promised was given; what Israel has not taken is Israel’s own deferred work, not Yahweh’s default. The verse is the load-bearing summary of the book’s theology of land.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        22,
        10,
        "",
        "a  great altar",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The altar by the Jordan — and what it almost cost.</strong> The trans-Jordanian tribes’ memorial-altar nearly precipitates the OT’s first major Israelite-on-Israelite war: the cis-Jordanian tribes mobilize for full <em>ḥerem</em> against their kin (22:11–12), assuming the altar is an apostate sacrifice site competing with Shiloh. Phinehas leads the delegation that resolves it through direct dialogue (22:13–34), a striking OT precedent for averting religious civil war through structured conversation rather than force. The episode embeds the Deuteronomistic insistence on cult centralization (Deut 12) at the level of inter-tribal politics: even a memorial altar, built explicitly not for sacrifice, is dangerous because it could be misread. The chapter is one of the OT’s most thoughtful treatments of how shared symbols can become the focal points of unintended conflict — and how communication, not force, defuses them.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        23,
        12,
        "",
        "make marriages with them",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The intermarriage warning.</strong> Joshua’s farewell explicitly forbids intermarriage with the surviving Canaanites — not on ethnic grounds but on theological ones, on the principle that intermarriage produces religious assimilation (cf. Deut 7:3–4). The principle will become one of the most charged topics of post-exilic Yahwism: Ezra 9–10 and Nehemiah 13 record forced divorces from foreign wives as the founding act of restored Yahwistic identity. The OT’s own counter-tradition is significant: Moses married a Cushite (Num 12:1), Boaz married Ruth the Moabite (canonized as Davidic ancestor), and the prophets of the Solomonic-and-after period frequently move between cultures. Joshua’s warning is practical theology rather than ethnic prescription, but its later weaponization in post-exilic communal definition demonstrates how a single verse can be re-applied to political ends across centuries. The verse is a foundational text in the OT debate over Israelite identity by descent vs. by covenant fidelity.",
        "User original",
    ),
